(or trying to read) a quantum mechanics textbook and then
attempting to make interesting comments about it at a party
with loud music in the background...  (um, nice typesetting,
eh?  what software package did he use?)  If somebody could
identify a particular important point from which to begin,
I'd shore appreciate it.

BUT, so that this message is not completely content-free, I will
try to kick off a nanotech thread by offering TWO glurgs*, one
speculative and one supplemental.

GLURG #1:  How to Build a Nanotech Doghouse in Three Easy Steps
(if this idea isn't laughed off the stage I may expand it into
an ExI-Essay someday...)

Tim mentioned Genetic Programming (GP) and I agree with his
assessment of its interest.  Remember Thought Habit #13 [from
a previous post of mine that sank like a stone, alas]:  juxtapose
new memes with old favorites and shake vigorously.  First (as a
free bonus), here's a 1-minute GP lecture:

  GP is like genetic algorithms (see Extropy #9) except that the
  genome is a computer program, represented as a parse tree
  (usually of a subset of LISP).  Recombination involves swapping
  subtrees between members of the population -- thus there is a
  function set (allowed functions) and a terminal set (constants),
  which is a bit silly since constants are usually treated as just
  zero-argument functions, but never mind that.  The idea is to
  evolve computer programs by comparing the computations performed
  with the desired functionality and using this comparison as a
  "fitness" measure of the program to see if it gets to have sex
  with another program or clone itself.  In this way, programs
  have been evolved to play tic-tac-toe, direct simulated robots
  to follow walls, and other useful tasks.  One point that is
  largely unrecognized is that the fitness functions of programs
  are REALLY to make themselves noticed, studied, and published
  by the researcher, but such subtleties are beyond the scope of
  this discussion.

Now, here's the idea (simplistic version):  our only data type
is molecules (more precisely, molecule sets).  Let the terminal
set be some initial batch of easily-produced molecules (or molecules
produced by some other process).  Let the members of the function
set be chemical reactions.  Each program then "outputs" products
of a complex chain of reactions.  Now, if we define the functional
properties of the molecule we want (e.g. binding to a particular
diamondoid-phosphorous-wedge-thingie), and if we have molecular
simulation software capable of checking to what degree the result
molecules satisfy the functional requirements, we should be able
to evolve the intermediate stages we'll need to perform sophisticated
nanotech construction operations.

Of course, we'd want to make extensions, to allow for other operations
such as centrifuging, heating, sending products through molecular
filters, and so on; but these functions would have the same form as
the other functions.  To make it right, the data type should probably
be some kind "concentration map" of the various chemical species...
and so on... the details are left as an exercise for the lurker.

There are also problems, such as the feasibility of the fitness
computation, how partial functionality is noticed, the shape of the
search space, and so on.  These problems are general to GP (and any
progress there would be directly applicable), and most of them simply
imply that it'll be difficult to take steps that are too large (e.g.
feed it "self-reproducing-assembler" and the periodic table and go to
lunch); but still it could be an important part of a functional design
system.

Comments?


GLURG #2:  What Eric D. Forgot to Tell You

There are two technologies that I didn't see mentioned in _Nanosystems_
that will probably end up being important parts of the nano-engineer's
toolbox:

A: LASERS

[Chu, Steven.  Laser Manipulation of Atoms and Particles. Science
 253 (1991), pp 861-865]

 You probably heard of this research regarding its ability to supercool
 molecules to 3 x 10^-6 degrees kelvin (that's right, within a few
 millionths of a degree of absolute zero -- eat your hearts out,
 cryonicists!).  Of at least as much interest is the ability to
 use these "optical traps" to hold single biological molecules in
 place and move them around; the researchers have "attached a polystyrene
 sphere to each end of a lambda phage DNA molecule via a biotin-strepavidin-
 biotin bridge" -- it's nice to see that for some nanomanipulation tasks
 there are alternatives to AFMs and STMs.

B: MOLECULAR EVOLUTION

[For a review, see Kauffman, Stuart A.  Applied Molecular Evolution.
 Journal of Theoretical Biology 157 (1992), pp. 1-7]

 [In other news, Kauffman has suggested that a finite number of enzymes
 may serve as a universal enzymatic toolbox, which could be quite
 important]

 It would be nice if proteins and other biological molecules that we
 want to use to perform specific tasks could be evolved in vitro
 instead of being designed.  For some classes of molecules this is
 in fact turning out to be possible.  Techiniques for cloning DNA,
 RNA, proteins, etc, have been developed, which implies that the following
 simple process can be used to evolve biomolecules that bind to particular
 sites:  First, generate a bunch of "random" molecules, then cause
 their concentrations to change in proportion to how well they perform
 the desired task (for example, by introducing large molecules with
 the desired binding sites on them and then filtering the results).
 Then clone whatever's left, and repeat this process over and over.
 Because multiple mutations are often necessary to turn an existing
 protein into one that performs the task at hand, the ability to
 search through a multitude of alternatives and amplify good matches
 in this way is very helpful.


Well, Tim, I hope you're satisfied -- I've used up my posting time
quota for the day... and I could have written six flames about
rationality in the time it took to write this ONE measely message!

derek
