Subject: PHIL/DANCE: Reincarnation vs. Migration to Silicon

>Just out of pure, perverse curiousity, how is transferring consciousness to
>silicon more falisfiable than transferring consciousness to another
>physical being?

Because in the former case, one can devise a set of tests that would
determine whether consciousness had been trasferred. After the transfer
process, real, measurable effects would be available for examination.
This is not the case for any proposed mechanism of reincarnation that
I've ever heard.

Even if you suppose that a person has memories of past events -- data
that could only have been obtained from the person who experienced them
-- that still does not prove that they *were* that person. There is no
way to connect the two individuals.  Given this supposition, one could
argue as easily for a kind of "race memory" ala Jung.

