>time.  I and each of the other six contributors lose access for one day,
>but Perry comes back on line.  This proposed reform would thus allow us see
>how culture evolves on a list where people are endowed with ASCII medicine.

Too byzantine even for this list!

Suppose Joe Average, who almost never posts, decides to "take out" Perry
Metzger for a week. The cost to Joe is minimal, as he seldom if ever posts.

Ah, I recall someone saying, we'll perhaps adjust the "take out times" for
posting frequency--or was it quantity? Joe Average builds up his
"firepower" by either posting a lot of little messages (perhaps taunts from
the Bible about how Jesus could walk on water and had invented reanimation
long before the cryonicists) or some very long ones (long sections from the
Hindu Vedas come to mind).

Ah, then we'll consider the "quality" of the posts.....

I enjoy these speculations about (somehow) creating a kind of "market" for
posting privileges...I just can't any plausible way to do it. Paying for
posts was once discussed--that would just stop some of us from sharing our
contributions with the list (is this what you want?). Setting quotas on
numbers of posts would put Joe Average, who rarely has anything to say, on
the same level with Perry Metzger, who has a lot to say (as an example).

The normal market approach is *not* to put quotas on writers or make *them*
pay for postings. The normal market approach is to have _editors_ who seek
out articles, either paid or unpaid, and then _charge_ subscription fees.
Could this work on this list? Probably not today.

-Tim May

