Well, fair enough to push me on this.  Actually I took pains not to *say*
anything more than that if there are any such concrete ways currently
available that this is probably where they are to be found.  But, yes,
I think that this hypothetical case is indeed true.
 
     [very cogent paragraph expressing toleration for reports on
     practical mystical experimentation, subject to reasonable
     constraints of rationality or replicability compressed into
     this space]

Let me say at the outset that I am not likely to be the source of any
first-hand discussion of practical techniques.  But this isn't the only
possible line of discussion.  I would be interested--though it may be of
marginal relevance to  the list--in discussion of possible interpretations 
of mystical data on consciousness in terms of modern theories of
consciousness and cognition.  In a certain sense the experiment is 
reproducible, in that different experimenters can
agree on a description of the phenomenological results.  (The
difference between this and experimental science being that her
the phenomenological facts are the only data, which limits
verification of intersubjective agreement).
     So their descriptions of their observations constitute data. 
If you can find any pattern in these data, you have something you
can then compare with your favorite theory of consciousness.  So,
for example, many meditation techniques--"clearing your mind",
reciting mantras, doing yoga or martial arts katas so as to focus
your attention on your body, ritual magick, and more--could be
described as having the same function, of pulling your
consciousness away from the word-stream and the stream of visual
and auditory percepts that are your normal phenomenological
environment.  OK, given a model of consciousness--Dennett's, say-
-can you make it make any predictions about what the effect of
such an exercise might be?


> Let's get specific:  You mention 'magickal experimentation'
> as one of your reasonable examples.  I choose that one because
> I haven't a clue what 'magick' is.  Your scholar is apparently
> to hope to be lucky enough to find
> 
> > a few people making systematic observations
> > that eventually might actually contribute to the basis of a science.
> 
> The implication is that this would apply to your 'magick' as
> well.  Could you clue me in a bit (I don't expect a detailed
> research paper, just some general hints) on what "systematic
> observations" have been done with 'magick' that we should
> pay attention to it?

     OK, first what I mean by "magick" is the use of ceremonial
ritual as a mystical exercise--the sort of thing explored and
expounded by Aleister Crowley.  In fact I don't know anything
first- or reliable second-hand about ritual or its effects.  (On
the basis of the few practioners of it that I've known and its
public proponents, like Crowley and Wilson, I tend to suspect
that taste for ritual is one of those extrovert things, like
football and idle conversation with strangers, that I'm
constitutionally unable to understand).  I put it prominently in
my list mostly as a provocation--there are people who tend to be
undercritical rather overcritical of harmless-seeming things like
yoga and meditation, and would gloss over what I was suggesting
with images of meditation as a relaxation technique and how good
yoga is for your back.
     I put it on the list on the basis of Robert Anton Wilson's
defense and advocacy of Crowley.  If you look at much by Crowley
you'll probably decide that if Wilson actually found any wheat in
there, he must have been a magician himself to dig it out of the
colorful but very fluffy chaff.  But I actually think he did. 
Magick in this sense is a sort of externalized meditation. 
Mystical techniques are all intended to teach you to take
conscious control of your phenomenological environment.  Most of
them involve internally directing your attention and
consciousness.  (A little more on this in another post).  Group
ritual works from outside by controlling your social, and
Crowleyian individual ceremonial by controlling your sensory,
environment.  It seems like a stupid idea to me, I admit, but I
can well believe that it can have an effect.


Scott DeLancey



