>From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>

>> Personally, I have no care about convincing you of
>> this -- as Ayn Rand has pointed out, the denial of reason is in some
>> sense its own punishment as those who deny reason in the end die because
>> of it.

>So, Perry -- why?

When you deny reason and reality, you make it hard for yourself to
function in the real world. If cause and effect cease to mean anything
for you, what stops you from walking in front of cars? If you can't
think or think that thinking is bad, you start having trouble managing
to make a living or keeping yourself fed.

"Atlas Shrugged" is rather badly written for my tastes, but it does
make a few good points (in its "yell in their faces" alegorical way)
-- one of them is that when men start denying reason the world, which
cares nothing for what people think, will become a far more dangerous
place to them. To the man who bothers to think, grabbing high-tension
wires on a whim is not a likely action, but to the man who denies
reason, it becomes a much more likely event. Although few of the
reason-denyers out there grab high tension wires since few of them
truly ban reason completely from their lives, they do similar things
that take longer to achieve the same results. Denying reality is never
a good thing -- reality has a way of creeping up and biting you in the
ass when you least expect it.

Perry


