>From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)

>>Carol Moore writes:
>>> It suddenly occured to me -- why is the idea of
>>> humans storing or transfering their consciousness to
>>> silicon chips taken seriously in some circles,
>>> while the idea of transfering consciousness to another
>>> physical being is not?
>>
>>Because the former is falsifiable while the latter is not.

>Just out of pure, perverse curiousity, how is transferring consciousness to
>silicon more falisfiable than transferring consciousness to another
>physical being?

In principle, both are falsifyable propositions. However, the
reincarnationists insist that the conditions for testing their
hypothesis be rigged in such a way that it becomes non-falsifyable.
This reminds me much of the problem with Mr. Singh's flying yogis --
they can only fly for the faithful, and since anyone could be declared
non-faithful it is impossible to test the hypothesis. It thus goes
into the same category as the invisible bunnies and all the other
non-falsifyable hypotheses. Now, were someone to claim that with the
use of a particular technique it was possible to produce complete
memory of a previous life from any test subject, including things like
memory of the ancient language the subject spoke and the like, it
would be possible to test the past-lives hypothesis, and thus it would
be possible to falsify it.

Perry

